# Review: .NET framework changes of the 0.17.0 DONTWAIT campaign — contract adaptation vs workaround vs spec gap

You are a codex sub-agent under a Claude supervisor doing a READ-ONLY conformance review. Do not edit any file except your deliverable; do not run gates that modify trees; never touch `core/**`, `bindings/**`, any `doc/spec/**`, `framework/doc/framework/**`, `doc/site/**`. Context: the framework worked end to end on Core 0.15/0.16 (baseline `seven-samples-green-v1` = `4bad5ac979`, 2026-08-29, all 7 samples green in 4 languages). Core 0.17.0 changed the contracts: SEND/REQUEST DONTWAIT return a wait token on BACKPRESSURED and the binding async terminal resubmits on WRITABLE (`50d77800f2`, D-B85 `7d8205a028`); `ZLINK_POLLCOMPLETION` is level-triggered and the public poller is the sole completion-drain owner; ROUTER submit to an unknown RID = `ZLINK_SUBMIT_NOT_FOUND`+`ENOENT`; ROUTER RID duplicate policy HANDOVER with reciprocal collapse by RID comparison (core socket README §4). The user's expectation: the ONLY framework changes needed are adaptations to those contract changes; every other change is suspect — either a legitimate pre-existing framework bug that the new Core exposed, or a workaround that hides a root cause (in Core, a binding, or the framework) and risks a spec gap. Your job: for EVERY commit listed below, read the diff (`git show <hash>`) and its worklog summary in `doc/plan/c016-worklog/*.md` (grep the hash), then classify it as exactly one of: (A) required 0.17.0 contract adaptation — cite the Core/binding spec clause (`core/doc/spec/**`, `bindings/doc/spec/**`) that requires it; (B) legitimate framework defect fix — cite the framework spec clause (`framework/doc/framework/common/spec/server/**`) the OLD code violated and explain why it was latent before 0.17.0; (C) workaround/mitigation — the change makes a symptom disappear without the cause being established (budget/timeout increases, retries, reordering "to be safe", fixture-only changes that hide runtime behaviour); name the most likely real cause and the evidence needed to confirm it; (D) spec gap — the change makes behaviour diverge from a spec clause (cite it) or invents a rule no spec has; (E) harness/build/sample-only, no runtime effect. PARITY CHECK is mandatory for every (B)/(C)/(D): find how the cpp implementation (and node) handles the same situation — cpp and node pass all gates — and state whether cpp/node needed an equivalent change (if cpp did NOT need it, explain precisely why dotnet/java did: a genuine structural difference, or a symptom of a different root cause). Verdict per commit: KEEP / RE-FIX AT ROOT (where) / REVERT. Be concrete: file:line, spec clause ids, and what the cpp code does. Deliverable: a Korean markdown report with one table row per commit (hash, one-line, class, spec clause, cpp/node parity finding, verdict) followed by a "재수정 권고" section ordered by risk, and a "스펙 gap 후보" section. 2 h cap.

## Commits to review (dotnet), oldest first
- `7e655e3703` framework/dotnet: transition runtime to the 0.16.0 pull-completion model (context: the adaptation baseline)
- `4644af9d03` framework/dotnet: poll SUB sockets without claiming completion ownership
- `4d263e66b9` framework: route DONTWAIT sends through the binding async path and drain WRITABLE on POLLOUT (dotnet part)
- `af7afd28e7` framework: port the cpp ZoneWorld crash-boundary fix to .NET/Java/Node (dotnet part)
- `b28eb24270` framework/dotnet: mesh admission follows the ROUTER handover contract (settle before submit, intent-owned transport) — the largest change (~300 lines in `ZLinkManagedMeshNode.cs`, `ZLinkMeshPeerAdmission.cs`, `ZLinkMeshPeer.cs`). Review each of its four parts separately: settle-before-submit (BeginReciprocalHandoverSettlement/ObserveReciprocalHandoverDisconnect), unilateral-Hello fallback + FindReadyOutboundCandidate/_readyOutboundEndpoints, relay RID→pair re-pin, DisconnectTransport endpoint-vs-RID rule. Key parity question: the cpp mesh node (`framework/languages/cpp/framework/src/runtime/mesh/raw_mesh_node_owner.cpp`, `mesh_node_runtime.cpp`) passes the same bilateral/handover/spot-route scenarios on the same Core — does cpp implement settlement before publishing Admitted, or does it never hit the problem because of a structural difference (e.g. cpp never dials back, or resends per spec §4 "Caller는 handover 뒤 다시 보낸다")? Is the .NET framework now doing something the spec assigns to Core (e.g. waiting for lane Disconnected before Admitted — spec clause?) — cite `03-mesh-node.ko.md:303-313`, `01-channel-topology.ko.md:457-469,688-700`, core README §4.
- `25952a76bc` framework/dotnet: ClientServer reconnect observes the physical close edge before re-registering the endpoint (+ generation-fenced retries, polling the socket during the wait). Parity: how does cpp's ClientServer client reconnect after a malformed pushed control (`channel_outbound_exchange.cpp` / client runtime)? Does it wait for a monitor edge? If cpp reconnects with a fixed delay and passes, why did dotnet need the edge wait — is the real cause the Core "same endpoint re-registration while the prior pipe retires" behaviour (D-086 family) that the framework now compensates for?
- `6b77ba013f` framework/dotnet tests: Canonical fixture owns every DEALER it creates (fixture) — confirm it hides nothing (the DEALER leak was the hang cause per `core-ctx-term-teardown-hang-summary.md`), and check the "RouteAdmission fixture ordering fix" hunk in the same commit: does it change which admission the test observes (weakened test)?
- `ebff5b3e1b` framework/dotnet: auto-connect reuses an already admitted inbound peer for the same RID/endpoint. Parity: cpp `connect_peer` behaviour when an admitted inbound peer with the same RID/endpoint exists.

Also list, for `ZLinkManagedMeshNode.cs`, every place where the .NET framework now makes a decision based on Core monitor events (ConnectionReady/Disconnected per lane) that cpp does not — that is where spec gaps hide.

